WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.143 --> 00:00:06.560
SPEAKER: As a
developer, it can be

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.410
difficult to get started
with new cloud technologies.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.370
Whether you're looking
to deploy a web app,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.450
set up a new database, or
run big data workloads,

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.090
it's often challenging to find
the right materials to help

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.650
you get up and running.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.500
This is where Google Cloud
quickstarts come into play.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.290
Quickstarts are quick
step-by-step tutorials

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.530
to help you get started with
new Google Cloud products.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.020
They typically cover
basic use cases, like how

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.080
to make your first API call or
deploy your first hello world

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.790
application.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.400
Quickstarts can cover several
different ways of interacting

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.230
with Google Cloud products.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.180
First, some quickstarts
show you how

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.640
to get things done from
the Google Cloud Console.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.280
This is where you can manage
your cloud resources directly

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.660
from a web portal.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.850
You can do things like
create new compute engine

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.060
virtual machine instances,
set up new databases,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.820
and check on the status
of deployed apps.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.770
You can also create
new projects,

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.560
set up billing, and
manage permissions.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.120
Second, there are
quickstarts that

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.459
show you how to use Google
Cloud command line tools,

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:08.360
like G Cloud, gsutil
and BQ, all of which

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.320
are part of the
Google Cloud SDK.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.200
G Cloud is a command
line tool that

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.420
allows you to do things
like create compute engine

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.930
virtual machine instances,
deploy web apps to App Engine,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.960
and set up Google
kubernetes engine clusters.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.940
Gsutil is the command
line tool that

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.250
allows you to interact
with cloud storage.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.130
You can do things like create
new buckets, upload file

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.130
objects and download
file objects.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:34.370
BQ is a command line tool for
interacting with BigQuery,

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.950
Google Cloud's enterprise
data warehouse designed

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.240
to help you ingest, store,
analyze and visualize big data.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.050
And finally, we have
quickstarts that

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.470
show you how to interact
with Google Cloud via code

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.720
using our client libraries.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.780
These quickstarts include
instructions and sample code

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.720
for programming languages,
like Python, node.js, Go, Java,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.540
Ruby, PHP, C-sharp, and C++.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.520
Let's take a look at
an example quickstart

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.980
for the Cloud Vision
API, which provides you

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.850
insights about your images using
the power of machine learning.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.340
First, there's the
Before You Begin section,

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.830
which shows you how to do
common tasks before you

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.770
use any product, such as
setting up a new project,

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:22.590
enabling billing, and
managing authentication.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.650
Next, you learn how to install
the Cloud Vision API client

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.830
library for the programming
language of your choice.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.230
And finally, there are
pieces of sample code

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.670
in different
programming languages

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.860
to show you how to use the
client libraries to call

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.740
the Cloud Vision API via code.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.530
For Python, you can just
copy and paste this code

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.120
into a new file called
main.py and run Python main.py

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.280
from your command
line interface.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.070
So where can you find
these quickstart tutorials?

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.170
You can find our full
collection of quickstarts

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.540
at cloud.google.com
/docs/quickstarts.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.420
Quickstarts are easy to find
in our product documentation.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.850
Here in the Cloud Vision
API documentation,

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.590
there's a section in
the left navigation

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.050
bar for quickstart tutorials.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.023
And finally, we also have video
versions of these quickstarts,

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:12.690
where we walk you through how to
get started with various Google

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.040
Cloud products.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.320
Check out the links in
the description below.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.610
And there you have it.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.470
Quickstarts are
easy-to-use tutorials

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.043
that show you step-by-step
how to get started.

00:03:22.043 --> 00:03:23.460
Make sure to click
the like button

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.310
and subscribe to the channel for
more getting started content.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.830
We're excited to
see what you build.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

